"Let $X$ be a connected simple graph, and let $c^{(X)}$ and $c^{(\\mathsf{Star}_n)}$ be multiplicity lists, with equal total capacity. Let the center of $\\mathsf{Star}_n$ have multiplicity\n\n$$\nk=c^{(\\mathsf{Star}_n)}_{\\varnothing}.\n$$\n\nA **$k$-bridge** is a bridge of the relevant multiplicity determined by the center multiplicity $k$. Let $\\operatorname{\\mathsf{FS_{m,m}}}(X,\\mathsf{Star}_n)$ be the double-multiplicity friends-and-strangers graph. **The bridge characterization conjecture.** The graph $\\operatorname{\\mathsf{FS_{m,m}}}(X,\\mathsf{Star}_n)$ is connected if and only if $X$ contains no $k$-bridge in which all vertices have multiplicity $1$. This would extend the paper's connectivity characterizations for single-multiplicity friends-and-strangers graphs to the double-multiplicity setting."